WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.144 --> 00:00:03.977
- Never gave up.

00:00:03.977 --> 00:00:04.810
- We didn't give up.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:05.866
- Continued to move forward.

00:00:05.866 --> 00:00:07.526
- Never ever ever give up.

00:00:07.526 --> 00:00:08.359
- Never give up.

00:00:08.359 --> 00:00:09.192
- Never give up.

00:00:09.192 --> 00:00:11.450
- Never ever ever give up.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:12.453
- Hello Believe nation.

00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:16.464
Today we're going to talk
about how to never give up.

00:00:16.464 --> 00:00:17.934
And as always guys as you're watching,

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:20.123
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:20.123 --> 00:00:21.730
please write it down
in the comments below.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.153
Put quotes around it as well,

00:00:23.153 --> 00:00:24.651
so others could be inspired.

00:00:24.651 --> 00:00:26.044
And when you write something down,

00:00:26.044 --> 00:00:30.211
you're much more likely to
remember it, so it helps you too.

00:00:31.122 --> 00:00:33.539
(slow music)

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.807
- Essentially and I would
say this to anybody,

00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:45.742
is that you know it's really,

00:00:45.742 --> 00:00:48.492
it's easy to embrace hopelessness

00:00:50.437 --> 00:00:52.967
when things seem insurmountable.

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:57.329
And yet, it's actually
just a matter of time

00:00:57.329 --> 00:00:58.867
until all of the elements come together

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:00.021
for things to be alright.

00:01:00.021 --> 00:01:04.104
I mean, I believe that,
most difficult situations

00:01:05.682 --> 00:01:08.420
will resolve themselves
if you are persistent

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.997
and if you don't give up entirely.

00:01:11.997 --> 00:01:13.558
And that's what I never
did, I never gave up.

00:01:13.558 --> 00:01:15.027
- We were forced in the beginning.

00:01:15.027 --> 00:01:16.642
I wish I could say we were geniuses

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:20.375
and say we're going to
start our own company.

00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:21.769
That's not what happened.

00:01:21.769 --> 00:01:24.015
In the beginning we went
to every single label

00:01:24.015 --> 00:01:27.568
and every single label
shut they door on us.

00:01:27.568 --> 00:01:31.208
The genius thing that we
did was we didn't give up.

00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:35.160
We didn't say, because
these guys, we used that

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.818
what do they know approach.

00:01:36.818 --> 00:01:39.576
We didn't give up at that point.

00:01:39.576 --> 00:01:42.114
I think that was the genius thing we did.

00:01:42.114 --> 00:01:43.607
We started selling our own CDs

00:01:43.607 --> 00:01:45.128
and we built our own buzz and then

00:01:45.128 --> 00:01:46.951
the record company came back to us.

00:01:46.951 --> 00:01:50.118
So now we had a different negotiation.

00:01:51.906 --> 00:01:55.815
It wasn't the same artist
label relationship.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:59.623
Now we retain ownership
in our own company.

00:01:59.623 --> 00:02:01.916
And it was the best thing for us.

00:02:01.916 --> 00:02:03.089
- [Stephen] The decline in my health

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:07.089
was this dark reminder
that time was against me.

00:02:08.335 --> 00:02:11.353
Yet despite the pressures in my family,

00:02:11.353 --> 00:02:15.175
I was determined to realize
a life long ambition,

00:02:15.175 --> 00:02:19.342
by writing a popular book about
how the universe had begun.

00:02:20.445 --> 00:02:23.165
I wanted the book to be read by millions

00:02:23.165 --> 00:02:25.298
of people around the world,

00:02:25.298 --> 00:02:28.954
like the best selling airport novel.

00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:30.759
- I did not think it would work.

00:02:30.759 --> 00:02:31.963
I did not think it would work

00:02:31.963 --> 00:02:34.399
because basically if you look at all

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:35.968
the other books in airports,

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:38.885
there are none like that, you know?

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:41.504
- [Stephen] However, I felt sure

00:02:41.504 --> 00:02:43.775
that the mass market would want to know

00:02:43.775 --> 00:02:46.829
about how the universe began.

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:50.412
By 1984, I had completed
the first chapter.

00:02:51.338 --> 00:02:54.062
I signed up with Peter and set to work

00:02:54.062 --> 00:02:57.892
completing my first draft of my book.

00:02:57.892 --> 00:02:59.865
I tried to simplify the physics

00:02:59.865 --> 00:03:02.303
as best I could and by the end

00:03:02.303 --> 00:03:06.470
I was pleased and felt it
was in really good shape.

00:03:07.523 --> 00:03:09.926
But Peter wasn't convinced.

00:03:09.926 --> 00:03:12.759
- I was pretty disappointed, yeah.

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.767
I thought this is going
to be really difficult.

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:18.488
- [Stephen] Lightning did indeed strike,

00:03:18.488 --> 00:03:22.488
but not in the way that
Peter and I were hoping.

00:03:23.551 --> 00:03:27.032
That summer I had taken
a break from rewriting,

00:03:27.032 --> 00:03:30.848
to travel to Switzerland on holiday.

00:03:30.848 --> 00:03:34.557
But while I was there, I
caught a chest infection

00:03:34.557 --> 00:03:36.662
that developed into pneumonia

00:03:36.662 --> 00:03:39.329
and quickly became very serious.

00:03:43.021 --> 00:03:45.724
I was put into a drug induced coma

00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:48.391
and onto a life support machine.

00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:52.041
The doctors thought I was so far gone

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:56.640
they offered (mumbles)
to turn off the machine.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:57.973
But she refused.

00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:04.733
Finally, Shane insisted that
I was flown back to Cambridge.

00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:08.900
The weeks of intensive care
were the darkest of my life.

00:04:09.876 --> 00:04:13.309
I felt I had always
fought my illness so hard

00:04:13.309 --> 00:04:17.059
that I was not prepared
to give in so easily.

00:04:18.818 --> 00:04:23.497
Slowly the drugs began to
work and the infection passed.

00:04:23.497 --> 00:04:25.203
But the surgeons had to perform

00:04:25.203 --> 00:04:28.220
a tracheotomy to allow me to breathe,

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.763
which made a small
incision in my wind pipe

00:04:31.763 --> 00:04:35.930
and connected me to a ventilator
via the hole in my throat.

00:04:38.102 --> 00:04:42.269
As a result, I was now robbed
of the ability to talk.

00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:49.321
I faced a life unable
to properly communicate.

00:04:49.321 --> 00:04:51.679
All hopes of finishing my book

00:04:51.679 --> 00:04:55.585
and perhaps even my
career, seemed to be over.

00:04:55.585 --> 00:04:57.851
I had enough movement in my right hand

00:04:57.851 --> 00:05:00.745
to be able to click the computer system

00:05:00.745 --> 00:05:03.162
and write the words I wanted.

00:05:04.663 --> 00:05:07.996
Finally I was free to communicate again.

00:05:09.744 --> 00:05:12.572
I was keen to make up the lost time

00:05:12.572 --> 00:05:16.111
that my illness had forced upon me.

00:05:16.111 --> 00:05:19.207
I had a stack of notes from Peter Guzzardi

00:05:19.207 --> 00:05:23.290
suggesting changes and
clarifications to my book.

00:05:24.308 --> 00:05:26.138
But I needed practical help with

00:05:26.138 --> 00:05:29.134
the rewrite at my end,
someone who could act

00:05:29.134 --> 00:05:30.467
as a go between.

00:05:33.801 --> 00:05:37.788
After months of work,
the rewrite was complete.

00:05:37.788 --> 00:05:39.374
None of us really knew whether

00:05:39.374 --> 00:05:44.307
the book would be liked and
would sell as we all hoped for.

00:05:44.307 --> 00:05:47.396
All we could do now was give it a title,

00:05:47.396 --> 00:05:51.315
A Brief History of Time,
send it off to the printers

00:05:51.315 --> 00:05:52.956
and wait.

00:05:52.956 --> 00:05:55.873
(fast paced music)

00:05:58.443 --> 00:06:00.149
But to everyone's surprise,

00:06:00.149 --> 00:06:02.649
the book sold copy after copy.

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:07.284
And very quickly book
shops were selling out.

00:06:07.284 --> 00:06:08.490
- When it hit the best seller list,

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:09.746
you're obviously surprised.

00:06:09.746 --> 00:06:11.177
It was a pleasant
surprise and it certainly

00:06:11.177 --> 00:06:13.342
was a surprise I don't (mumbles),

00:06:13.342 --> 00:06:15.763
maybe I'm wrong, we'll say
oh yes we knew all along

00:06:15.763 --> 00:06:17.929
this was going to be a major hit.

00:06:17.929 --> 00:06:19.750
- I had no expectations it would be

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.617
the number one best
selling book in the world.

00:06:22.617 --> 00:06:25.667
I mean, not just here
but Germany, Slovenia,

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:28.126
France, Italy, everywhere in the world

00:06:28.126 --> 00:06:30.316
there was the hope that someone

00:06:30.316 --> 00:06:32.609
had found the mystery of life.

00:06:32.609 --> 00:06:33.962
- And from them on it was just a race

00:06:33.962 --> 00:06:37.795
to keep the book in print
and marching towards

00:06:38.995 --> 00:06:40.828
a million copies sold.

00:06:41.863 --> 00:06:44.347
- It was very gratifying.

00:06:44.347 --> 00:06:49.058
In the 38 years that I've
been in this business,

00:06:49.058 --> 00:06:52.058
I don't think I have ever had a book

00:06:52.904 --> 00:06:56.950
that stayed at the top of
the best sell list that long.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.608
- I was amazing at how well it did.

00:06:59.608 --> 00:07:02.499
I think it worked, he inspired people.

00:07:02.499 --> 00:07:04.717
He gave people some overall sense

00:07:04.717 --> 00:07:06.538
of the birth of the universe.

00:07:06.538 --> 00:07:09.535
And it made this subject become

00:07:09.535 --> 00:07:12.176
a subject of conversation among

00:07:12.176 --> 00:07:15.072
people in all walks of life.

00:07:15.072 --> 00:07:16.417
- [News Reporter] (mumbles)
the Stephen Hawkings book,

00:07:16.417 --> 00:07:18.958
The Brief History of Time, an unlikely

00:07:18.958 --> 00:07:21.110
but successful publishing, phenomenal.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.894
- [News Reporter 2] A Brief
History of Time has sold

00:07:22.894 --> 00:07:24.618
about eight million copies.

00:07:24.618 --> 00:07:26.250
- [News Reporter 3] A popular
book about his theories

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.949
is already topping the
American best sellers list.

00:07:28.949 --> 00:07:30.354
- [News Reporter 4] The hugely successful,

00:07:30.354 --> 00:07:34.352
A Brief History of Time
by Stephen Hawking.

00:07:34.352 --> 00:07:35.990
- [Stephen] A Brief History of Time

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:39.229
stayed in the best seller
list for over four years

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:41.715
and entered into the
Guinness book of records

00:07:41.715 --> 00:07:42.980
for dong so.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.147
To date, over 10 million
copies had been sold worldwide.

00:07:55.144 --> 00:07:56.804
Over the next few years,

00:07:56.804 --> 00:08:00.258
a lot of fuss was made about my book.

00:08:00.258 --> 00:08:03.666
I became famous nationally
and around the world

00:08:03.666 --> 00:08:08.043
as it was translated into
40 different languages.

00:08:08.043 --> 00:08:09.503
- One of the greatest pieces of advice

00:08:09.503 --> 00:08:11.472
I've ever gotten in my
life was from my mom

00:08:11.472 --> 00:08:13.869
when I was a little kid.

00:08:13.869 --> 00:08:16.702
There was a kid who was
bugging me in school.

00:08:16.702 --> 00:08:19.891
And she said, okay I'm going
to tell you what to do.

00:08:19.891 --> 00:08:21.840
She said the kid's bugging you,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.007
he puts his hands on you,
you pick up the nearest rock

00:08:27.072 --> 00:08:28.383
or whatever you can get your hands on

00:08:28.383 --> 00:08:29.740
and you lay him out.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.990
(laughing)

00:08:35.709 --> 00:08:36.619
Then I did.

00:08:36.619 --> 00:08:38.395
(laughing)

00:08:38.395 --> 00:08:40.294
And I felt better.

00:08:40.294 --> 00:08:42.164
And it worked.

00:08:42.164 --> 00:08:44.144
(laughing)

00:08:44.144 --> 00:08:47.627
So for me, that was a great lesson

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:50.710
because someone had invaded my space,

00:08:52.167 --> 00:08:56.167
invaded my being and was
doing something against

00:08:58.239 --> 00:09:00.101
me that I didn't want to be done.

00:09:00.101 --> 00:09:04.268
So I took control of the
situation and I ran with it.

00:09:07.762 --> 00:09:10.429
And many years later after being

00:09:12.806 --> 00:09:16.806
turned into a product by
a very huge corporation

00:09:19.155 --> 00:09:21.070
that had their hands all over me

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.153
and I couldn't escape it.

00:09:26.798 --> 00:09:31.019
I promised myself that I
would continue to move forward

00:09:31.019 --> 00:09:35.186
and do my best not to compromise
in any way what so ever,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:40.053
not allow any one to put their hands on me

00:09:40.053 --> 00:09:42.034
and effect me in that way.

00:09:42.034 --> 00:09:45.375
So that's the best advice
I think I could give,

00:09:45.375 --> 00:09:46.840
is just to keep moving forward

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.174
and don't give a (beep)
about what anybody thinks.

00:09:49.174 --> 00:09:52.952
Just keep moving forward and
do what you have to do for you.

00:09:52.952 --> 00:09:54.971
- Practice faithful persistence

00:09:54.971 --> 00:09:57.301
means never ever ever give up.

00:09:57.301 --> 00:10:02.282
Chicken Soup for the Soul was
rejected by 144 publishers.

00:10:02.282 --> 00:10:04.400
144 publishers said we
don't want to publish

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.107
a collection of short stories,

00:10:06.107 --> 00:10:08.401
the title is stupid,
Chicken Soup for the Soul,

00:10:08.401 --> 00:10:09.401
what does that mean?

00:10:09.401 --> 00:10:12.690
People don't buy collections
of short stories,

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.249
there's never been successful
chain of doing that.

00:10:16.249 --> 00:10:18.832
So literally we spent 14 months

00:10:19.772 --> 00:10:21.312
trying to find a publisher.

00:10:21.312 --> 00:10:23.362
And we go to New York with our agent

00:10:23.362 --> 00:10:24.686
and eventually our agent gave us

00:10:24.686 --> 00:10:27.031
the book back and said I can't sell this.

00:10:27.031 --> 00:10:29.452
So we went to the National
Book Sellers Association

00:10:29.452 --> 00:10:31.067
convention down in Anaheim.

00:10:31.067 --> 00:10:34.242
We went from booth to booth
to booth for three days.

00:10:34.242 --> 00:10:37.394
And there were 4000
publishers represented there.

00:10:37.394 --> 00:10:38.544
And we go, would you publish our book,

00:10:38.544 --> 00:10:39.377
would you publish our book,

00:10:39.377 --> 00:10:40.431
would you publish our book?

00:10:40.431 --> 00:10:42.331
And we got no no no no no.

00:10:42.331 --> 00:10:43.717
It was late on the third day,

00:10:43.717 --> 00:10:45.153
that a little publisher from Florida,

00:10:45.153 --> 00:10:47.171
after we've gotten over 144 rejections,

00:10:47.171 --> 00:10:49.052
said we'll read it.

00:10:49.052 --> 00:10:50.752
And then we said, well how many copies

00:10:50.752 --> 00:10:52.557
do you think we'll sell, after they said

00:10:52.557 --> 00:10:53.793
yeah we'll buy it a couple weeks later,

00:10:53.793 --> 00:10:55.076
they said we'll publish it.

00:10:55.076 --> 00:10:58.484
And they said, maybe
20,000 if we're lucky.

00:10:58.484 --> 00:11:00.045
And we said, our goal is to sell

00:11:00.045 --> 00:11:02.604
a million and a half in a year and a half.

00:11:02.604 --> 00:11:03.859
Now have you ever told someone your dream

00:11:03.859 --> 00:11:05.171
and had them laugh out loud at you.

00:11:05.171 --> 00:11:07.046
(laughing)

00:11:07.046 --> 00:11:09.064
He laughed out loud.

00:11:09.064 --> 00:11:12.651
Now if we had given up after
100 publishers had said no,

00:11:12.651 --> 00:11:15.014
I wouldn't be standing here today.

00:11:15.014 --> 00:11:16.358
It wouldn't have happened.

00:11:16.358 --> 00:11:18.931
And so you've got to be
willing to persevere.

00:11:18.931 --> 00:11:20.894
Because we didn't take no.

00:11:20.894 --> 00:11:23.576
See when the world says no, you say next.

00:11:23.576 --> 00:11:25.554
Let's practice that, if
i say no, you say ...

00:11:25.554 --> 00:11:26.624
- [Audience] Next.

00:11:26.624 --> 00:11:27.457
- Absolutely.

00:11:27.457 --> 00:11:29.240
There's only five or six
billion people out there

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.001
you can go and ask, right?.

00:11:31.001 --> 00:11:32.969
There's over 4000 publishers just here

00:11:32.969 --> 00:11:34.791
in the state of California.

00:11:34.791 --> 00:11:37.201
So what we have is this
endless possibility,

00:11:37.201 --> 00:11:38.727
but you have to persevere.

00:11:38.727 --> 00:11:41.210
It's as if the universe
tests our commitment.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.190
And so today we have 147 titles in print,

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.290
we've sold over 115 million copies,

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.596
we're in 47 languages and over

00:11:48.596 --> 00:11:51.167
a billion dollars worth of
Chicken Soup for the Soul

00:11:51.167 --> 00:11:52.548
books have been sold worldwide.

00:11:52.548 --> 00:11:55.068
So if you're willing to
decide what you want,

00:11:55.068 --> 00:11:58.847
believe it's possible,
consciously envision the future,

00:11:58.847 --> 00:12:00.559
feel the feelings of
what it would feel like

00:12:00.559 --> 00:12:02.539
if you would achieve this goal.

00:12:02.539 --> 00:12:05.511
You could have anything
and then you take action.

00:12:05.511 --> 00:12:07.468
Both obvious actions and aspired action,

00:12:07.468 --> 00:12:09.946
you can do anything.

00:12:09.946 --> 00:12:12.553
- If you're convinced
that you're going to fail

00:12:12.553 --> 00:12:16.059
or fall or get hurt, that
will come to fruition

00:12:16.059 --> 00:12:19.554
because that's what you're visualizing.

00:12:19.554 --> 00:12:21.666
I was willing to get hurt along the way,

00:12:21.666 --> 00:12:25.039
but I didn't visualize myself
getting hurt along the way.

00:12:25.039 --> 00:12:28.726
And I think that's what
stops a lot of people

00:12:28.726 --> 00:12:30.281
and that's really what
separates a lot of people

00:12:30.281 --> 00:12:34.351
from being world champions or from being

00:12:34.351 --> 00:12:35.297
as good as they can

00:12:35.297 --> 00:12:36.890
because they stop at some point

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:40.510
in saying, oh no I am not capable of this,

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:42.822
or I'll probably get hurt doing this.

00:12:42.822 --> 00:12:45.092
And if you approach anything
with that hesitation,

00:12:45.092 --> 00:12:46.343
you're going to fail.

00:12:46.343 --> 00:12:49.492
I always approached it
like, this is going to work,

00:12:49.492 --> 00:12:51.611
I'm going to figure this out,

00:12:51.611 --> 00:12:55.028
even if it takes me hundreds of attempts.

00:12:58.493 --> 00:13:01.574
The idea of falling, getting back up again

00:13:01.574 --> 00:13:02.841
that didn't really bother me at all.

00:13:02.841 --> 00:13:05.041
- Unfortunately there are people with

00:13:05.041 --> 00:13:06.463
the talent that don't have the money.

00:13:06.463 --> 00:13:08.342
I kind of look at it like if any of you

00:13:08.342 --> 00:13:09.934
have seen Cool Run Ins,
when they try to raise

00:13:09.934 --> 00:13:12.558
money to go above sand and all the people

00:13:12.558 --> 00:13:14.317
that they approached just laughed at them.

00:13:14.317 --> 00:13:16.139
And it's the same for us, you know,

00:13:16.139 --> 00:13:18.648
when my dad would say,
my son's going to be

00:13:18.648 --> 00:13:19.884
the first Platform one driver,

00:13:19.884 --> 00:13:22.067
everyone kind of thought, yeah right.

00:13:22.067 --> 00:13:25.178
But you know you kept
going and it happened,

00:13:25.178 --> 00:13:27.964
we got there so never give up.

00:13:27.964 --> 00:13:29.964
- It's Tyler and DJ Ski.

00:13:31.248 --> 00:13:33.415
And that was the carnival.

00:13:35.261 --> 00:13:37.316
- The carnival was tight.

00:13:37.316 --> 00:13:38.149
- How do you feel?

00:13:38.149 --> 00:13:38.982
It's the fourth one?

00:13:38.982 --> 00:13:39.987
- Mhmm.

00:13:39.987 --> 00:13:41.582
- Do you think that it would grow

00:13:41.582 --> 00:13:42.807
to be something this massive?

00:13:42.807 --> 00:13:46.808
- No we was going to cancel
it this year but we didn't.

00:13:46.808 --> 00:13:49.552
We was literally like
(beep) it and then we did it

00:13:49.552 --> 00:13:50.987
and then a lot of people came.

00:13:50.987 --> 00:13:52.647
- Why were you going to cancel it?

00:13:52.647 --> 00:13:53.480
- I don't know.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:55.340
We couldn't find anyone to perform.

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:57.006
It was just such like a,

00:13:57.006 --> 00:14:00.756
'cause we're specific
on who we wanted there,

00:14:02.052 --> 00:14:02.885
things like that.

00:14:02.885 --> 00:14:06.906
And we couldn't come up
with the perfect thing

00:14:06.906 --> 00:14:08.636
and it was just a depressing time.

00:14:08.636 --> 00:14:09.779
And we was about to say (beep) it,

00:14:09.779 --> 00:14:11.929
until we was like (beep)
it, what are we doing?

00:14:11.929 --> 00:14:13.654
Where's our spirit?

00:14:13.654 --> 00:14:17.154
And we ran it back and it was our biggest,

00:14:19.482 --> 00:14:22.396
we dummy doubled up since last year.

00:14:22.396 --> 00:14:23.304
It was crazy.

00:14:23.304 --> 00:14:24.137
- Good question.

00:14:24.137 --> 00:14:25.833
At what age should one
give up on their dream?

00:14:25.833 --> 00:14:27.447
Never.

00:14:27.447 --> 00:14:29.723
(mumbles) said this, Henry Ford said this,

00:14:29.723 --> 00:14:31.658
Rockefeller said this,
you're only as young

00:14:31.658 --> 00:14:33.364
or as old as your dreams.

00:14:33.364 --> 00:14:34.882
And as long as you still have dreams

00:14:34.882 --> 00:14:37.843
and visions and goals,
they keep you young.

00:14:37.843 --> 00:14:40.081
It's the most amazing darn thing.

00:14:40.081 --> 00:14:41.416
One of the great stories is

00:14:41.416 --> 00:14:44.276
the story of Kentucky Fried Chicken.

00:14:44.276 --> 00:14:47.759
Once upon a time, there was
a little boy named Harland

00:14:47.759 --> 00:14:50.303
and Harland was taught by his mother

00:14:50.303 --> 00:14:53.385
a special recipe down
south in the United States,

00:14:53.385 --> 00:14:55.029
on how to make chicken.

00:14:55.029 --> 00:14:56.682
When he grew up he got a job as

00:14:56.682 --> 00:14:59.608
a short order cook in a small cafe.

00:14:59.608 --> 00:15:01.867
And he asked his boss if his boss

00:15:01.867 --> 00:15:04.205
would be interested in
this chicken recipe.

00:15:04.205 --> 00:15:05.934
And his boss said, sure throw it together,

00:15:05.934 --> 00:15:07.550
see what the people say.

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:08.868
Turned out that everybody liked

00:15:08.868 --> 00:15:11.062
this special chicken recipe with all

00:15:11.062 --> 00:15:13.314
these secret herbs and spices.

00:15:13.314 --> 00:15:14.736
Well for the rest of his career,

00:15:14.736 --> 00:15:16.793
right up 'til he was 65 years old,

00:15:16.793 --> 00:15:18.766
he either worked in little cafes

00:15:18.766 --> 00:15:21.199
or sometimes he owned a little cafe.

00:15:21.199 --> 00:15:23.916
When he was 65 he had a little cafe

00:15:23.916 --> 00:15:26.064
and he was making his chicken

00:15:26.064 --> 00:15:29.924
and he had people coming from
all around to eat his chicken.

00:15:29.924 --> 00:15:32.583
And the highways
department built a highway

00:15:32.583 --> 00:15:36.333
past his little restaurant,
his little chicken place

00:15:36.333 --> 00:15:37.736
and put him out of business.

00:15:37.736 --> 00:15:40.593
They cut off the road so
nobody could get to it.

00:15:40.593 --> 00:15:41.987
About the same time he received

00:15:41.987 --> 00:15:45.864
a social security check, his
first social security check

00:15:45.864 --> 00:15:47.472
at the age of 65.

00:15:47.472 --> 00:15:49.062
And he became angry.

00:15:49.062 --> 00:15:51.977
He said, I'm 65 years
old but I'm not ready

00:15:51.977 --> 00:15:54.531
to give up on my dreams.

00:15:54.531 --> 00:15:56.230
What do I have to sell?

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:57.689
And he realized the one thing

00:15:57.689 --> 00:16:00.486
that had kept him going all those years,

00:16:00.486 --> 00:16:02.240
the main stay of his little career

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:06.407
was his mothers recipe of
17 or 18 herbs and spices.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.390
So he put them together in little bags.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:12.020
One bag per chicken.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.793
And he put his pots and pans in

00:16:13.793 --> 00:16:16.469
the back of his old car
and he started driving

00:16:16.469 --> 00:16:18.263
up and down the Eastern seaboard,

00:16:18.263 --> 00:16:20.931
going from cafe to cafe to cafe,

00:16:20.931 --> 00:16:24.485
to ask them if they would
pay him five cents a bag

00:16:24.485 --> 00:16:27.862
for his magical combination
of herbs and spices.

00:16:27.862 --> 00:16:30.568
He called on more than 1000 restaurants,

00:16:30.568 --> 00:16:33.795
sleeping in his car, week after week,

00:16:33.795 --> 00:16:37.704
and finally he came across a
little restaurant in Toronto

00:16:37.704 --> 00:16:40.261
that agreed to pay him five cents a bag,

00:16:40.261 --> 00:16:41.634
for his herbs and spices.

00:16:41.634 --> 00:16:43.036
This is back in the 60s.

00:16:43.036 --> 00:16:45.955
And because of exchange controls,

00:16:45.955 --> 00:16:48.506
they had to give the
money to the Red Cross.

00:16:48.506 --> 00:16:52.332
That was the beginning of the
Kentucky Fried Chicken empire.

00:16:52.332 --> 00:16:55.090
He began to sell this
recipe more and more,

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:57.846
people began to put it on their signs,

00:16:57.846 --> 00:16:59.612
a major corporation came along

00:16:59.612 --> 00:17:02.392
and said we could make
a real business of this.

00:17:02.392 --> 00:17:04.868
So they embraced him,
they built the franchise

00:17:04.868 --> 00:17:07.402
that made him one of the best
known people in the world.

00:17:07.402 --> 00:17:08.789
When he died seven years later,

00:17:08.789 --> 00:17:11.051
he was worth more than 50 million dollars,

00:17:11.051 --> 00:17:13.350
because he never gave up on his dreams.

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:16.297
So I don't know if you're
65 and broke and living

00:17:16.297 --> 00:17:19.275
in your car with pots
and pans with no money,

00:17:19.275 --> 00:17:22.962
but wherever you are, you
never give up on your dreams.

00:17:22.962 --> 00:17:24.960
- Stay in front of the competition,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.300
because there's two ways in this industry.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.651
You move it or it moves you.

00:17:29.651 --> 00:17:31.055
And I've seen so many sad stories

00:17:31.055 --> 00:17:35.565
across the decade where
chefs have just got lazy.

00:17:35.565 --> 00:17:40.430
Got lazy, given up and lost
that hunger to be competitive,

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.166
because it's the best job in the world.

00:17:42.166 --> 00:17:44.023
That's not really a job, it's a passion,

00:17:44.023 --> 00:17:46.781
because when you're rubbing shoulders.

00:17:46.781 --> 00:17:48.369
And here we are now, 10 years later,

00:17:48.369 --> 00:17:50.211
and I've got a restaurant
next door to Jamie Oliver's

00:17:50.211 --> 00:17:53.223
and we're both literally two meters apart.

00:17:53.223 --> 00:17:55.615
Phenomenal chef ...

00:17:55.615 --> 00:17:58.048
And we keep each other on our toes.

00:17:58.048 --> 00:18:00.614
So that's exciting for me and that's been

00:18:00.614 --> 00:18:02.853
the one key issue across the 10 years

00:18:02.853 --> 00:18:05.136
of filming Kitchen Nightmares,

00:18:05.136 --> 00:18:07.136
never ever ever give up.

00:18:08.107 --> 00:18:09.467
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:18:09.467 --> 00:18:11.201
I'd love to know what did you think.

00:18:11.201 --> 00:18:13.560
What was the biggest most important lesson

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:14.696
that you learned from this video

00:18:14.696 --> 00:18:16.543
that you are going to apply immediately

00:18:16.543 --> 00:18:18.625
to your life or your business.

00:18:18.625 --> 00:18:19.771
Leave it down in the comments below

00:18:19.771 --> 00:18:21.466
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:18:21.466 --> 00:18:23.141
I'm also curious, what
are some of the strategies

00:18:23.141 --> 00:18:25.692
that you use to never give up?

00:18:25.692 --> 00:18:26.932
What do you want to add to this video

00:18:26.932 --> 00:18:28.522
that might help other entrepreneurs

00:18:28.522 --> 00:18:30.643
who are considering giving
up in their business?

00:18:30.643 --> 00:18:32.081
Please leave it in the comments.

00:18:32.081 --> 00:18:33.831
And finally I want to
give a quick shout out

00:18:33.831 --> 00:18:35.808
to the Auckland Council Library.

00:18:35.808 --> 00:18:36.808
Thank you so much for picking up

00:18:36.808 --> 00:18:38.901
a copy of my book, Your One Word

00:18:38.901 --> 00:18:40.434
and having it in your library.

00:18:40.434 --> 00:18:42.114
I really really really appreciate it.

00:18:42.114 --> 00:18:43.599
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:18:43.599 --> 00:18:44.714
I believe in you.

00:18:44.714 --> 00:18:46.636
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:18:46.636 --> 00:18:48.487
and whatever your one word is.

00:18:48.487 --> 00:18:50.904
Much love, I'll see you soon.

